WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.640
Michael Myers isn't the only one cutting deep.&nbsp;
From human wall hangings to glasses of chocolate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:12.000
milk, "Halloween Ends" is filled with easy-to-miss&nbsp;
details and Easter eggs. Warning: spoilers ahead!

00:00:12.120 --> 00:00:16.980
In the movie's tragic and terrifying opening,&nbsp;
the hopeful prospective college student Corey&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.980 --> 00:00:21.780
Cunningham babysits local Haddonfield child&nbsp;
Jeremy Allen. Before the horror begins,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.780 --> 00:00:24.590
Corey and Jeremy are hanging&nbsp;
out and having a good time.

00:00:24.767 --> 00:00:28.307
"It's Halloween. We're gonna&nbsp;
have a good time tonight."

00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:33.000
Against the wishes of the Allens, Corey is&nbsp;
being a little lax and letting Jeremy watch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:37.020
scary movies with him. On that Halloween&nbsp;
Night, it's John Carpenter's "The Thing."

00:00:37.020 --> 00:00:40.980
Not only is the scene of these two watching&nbsp;
"The Thing" an obvious nod to Carpenter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.980 --> 00:00:45.180
director of "The Thing" and the original&nbsp;
"Halloween," but it has another layer to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.180 --> 00:00:50.040
it as well. In the 1978 "Halloween," there is&nbsp;
a scene of Laurie and the kid she is looking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.040 --> 00:00:55.080
after watching "The Thing." Of course, they&nbsp;
aren't watching Carpenter's 1982 masterpiece,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.080 --> 00:01:00.060
since he hadn't made it yet. The movie shown&nbsp;
in the original was 1951's "The Thing from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.060 --> 00:01:04.860
Another World," which Carpenter adapted and&nbsp;
reimagined for the '80s. Pretty fitting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.860 --> 00:01:09.827
considering the new "Halloween" trilogy is&nbsp;
also a reimagining of the Michael Myers story.

00:01:10.020 --> 00:01:14.100
Corey Cunningham becomes an outcast&nbsp;
after the night he accidentally kills&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:18.420
Jeremy Allen. On the night of the accident,&nbsp;
Corey even had a chance to drink a beer but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.420 --> 00:01:22.440
opts for chocolate milk to accompany his&nbsp;
dessert. Ultimately he never got a chance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.440 --> 00:01:26.820
to drink the milk. Years later, he buys&nbsp;
chocolate milk at a gas station. Afterward,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.820 --> 00:01:30.780
a group of teens asks him to buy them&nbsp;
beer, once again bringing Corey's drink&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.780 --> 00:01:35.580
of choice into question. But why exactly is&nbsp;
chocolate milk important to "Halloween Ends"?

00:01:35.580 --> 00:01:39.900
There are a couple of themes that could tie&nbsp;
into why Corey drinks chocolate milk. For one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.900 --> 00:01:45.060
it makes him different from others, reinforcing&nbsp;
his feeling of being an outcast. But his taste&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.060 --> 00:01:50.220
for chocolate milk, a drink usually associated&nbsp;
with children, is the important part here. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.220 --> 00:01:54.720
shows that on the inside, he is still more&nbsp;
of a child than he is an adult. Corey is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.720 --> 00:01:59.700
scared kid who never had a chance to grow up, and&nbsp;
Michael Myers preys on this to unleash his evil.

00:02:00.180 --> 00:02:04.380
Since this is "Halloween Ends," the story&nbsp;
of Laurie and Michael, appropriately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.380 --> 00:02:09.900
comes to a conclusion. As such, the movie pays&nbsp;
appropriate homage to where the story began. One&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.900 --> 00:02:15.000
visual callback that works on multiple levels is&nbsp;
just one of many references to John Carpenter's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:20.820
low-budget 1978 horror classic, a moment&nbsp;
where Corey Cunningham imitates Michael Myers.

00:02:20.820 --> 00:02:26.100
Corey becomes a sort of disciple of Michael,&nbsp;
enacting murders in Haddonfield after a chance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.100 --> 00:02:30.900
meeting with Michael in the sewers. But the&nbsp;
earliest visual hint that Corey is going down this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.900 --> 00:02:36.360
dark path is when he imitates the slasher villain,&nbsp;
peeking out from behind a bush. Eagle-eyed fans of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.360 --> 00:02:41.160
the original "Halloween" will recognize this as a&nbsp;
reference to an early shot in that film of Michael&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.160 --> 00:02:46.500
Myers in the exact same position. It's one of the&nbsp;
first glimpses we get of the character, as Laurie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.500 --> 00:02:51.540
walks down the sidewalk on an otherwise completely&nbsp;
normal fall day. The shot of Michael peeking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.540 --> 00:02:55.857
out from behind a hedge has become an iconic&nbsp;
image from the very first movie in the series.

00:02:56.040 --> 00:03:00.900
Sondra Dickerson is a minor character who winds&nbsp;
up surviving all three modern "Halloween" movies.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.900 --> 00:03:05.040
There was some speculation over whether or&nbsp;
not Sondra had died in the scene where Michael&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:09.540
attacked her and her husband in "Halloween&nbsp;
Kills." In that film, Sondra gets one of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.540 --> 00:03:14.280
most grotesque, painful encounters with Michael&nbsp;
Myers, watching him attack and kill her husband&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.280 --> 00:03:19.740
and standing in terror as he puts a tube light&nbsp;
in her neck. Now, in "Halloween Ends," we see she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.740 --> 00:03:24.300
has survived, but has been rendered disabled&nbsp;
by Michael's attack and is in a wheelchair.

00:03:24.300 --> 00:03:27.900
In one scene, Sondra's friend Stacy&nbsp;
confronts Laurie in a parking lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.900 --> 00:03:32.460
and blames her for her friend's condition&nbsp;
and the death of Sondra's husband. Later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.460 --> 00:03:37.320
when the town is carrying Michael to his final&nbsp;
resting place, we get a brief shot showing us&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.320 --> 00:03:42.480
that Sondra is still around. She survived the&nbsp;
events of "Halloween Ends," making her one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.480 --> 00:03:46.620
the lucky few throughout the franchise to&nbsp;
survive an encounter with Michael Myers.

00:03:46.620 --> 00:03:51.480
A great little trick in scary movies is when the&nbsp;
slasher or monster is lurking in the background&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.480 --> 00:03:56.880
of a shot, almost imperceptible. Terrifying&nbsp;
if done right, this trick can frighten and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.880 --> 00:04:01.500
foreshadow future events for those who notice.&nbsp;
It can also add rewatch value for anyone who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.500 --> 00:04:05.640
missed it the first time. When it's pulled off in&nbsp;
"Halloween Ends," it also has a deeper meaning,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.640 --> 00:04:09.300
since this is a trope the series&nbsp;
uses constantly with Michael Myers.

00:04:09.300 --> 00:04:14.460
In one sequence, Corey lures a cop who used to&nbsp;
date Allyson to Michael Myers' hiding place in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.460 --> 00:04:19.440
a sewer underneath Haddonfield. When "Halloween&nbsp;
Ends" first pulls this trick, you see Michael in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.440 --> 00:04:24.240
the background, foreshadowing the man's fate.&nbsp;
However, in a similar shot almost immediately&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.240 --> 00:04:28.500
after the first, we now see Corey lurking in&nbsp;
the background of the shot in the same way.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.500 --> 00:04:33.730
It's another hint of where his arc is going and&nbsp;
the similarities he is developing with Michael.

00:04:33.730 --> 00:04:36.223
"Show me how to do it."

00:04:36.223 --> 00:04:39.960
Every slasher movie needs a character&nbsp;
who exists just for the purpose of being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.960 --> 00:04:43.200
senselessly murdered. That appears&nbsp;
to be the purpose of Nurse Deb,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.200 --> 00:04:47.520
Allyson's annoying coworker in "Halloween&nbsp;
Ends." When it's shown that she is sleeping&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.520 --> 00:04:51.540
with their boss, Dr. Mathis, which is why&nbsp;
Deb received a promotion over Allyson,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.540 --> 00:04:55.680
Michael Myers and his protégé show up&nbsp;
for some good old-fashioned slashing.

00:04:55.680 --> 00:05:00.540
While Mathis is taken care of by Corey outside,&nbsp;
Deb is trapped inside the house with Michael.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.540 --> 00:05:05.400
When she is killed, Michael pins her to the&nbsp;
wall. It's a direct callback to a kill from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.400 --> 00:05:10.500
the 1978 original. In "Halloween," Michael&nbsp;
kills Bob by stabbing him and pinning him to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.500 --> 00:05:15.120
a cabinet with a kitchen knife. Deb meets a&nbsp;
similar fate, albeit stabbed in a different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.120 --> 00:05:19.860
place. "Halloween Ends" even puts the cherry&nbsp;
on top by having Michael childishly cock his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.860 --> 00:05:24.337
head to the side in wonder or curiosity&nbsp;
the same way he does in the original.

00:05:24.480 --> 00:05:27.180
In the original "Halloween,"&nbsp;
during their first encounter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.180 --> 00:05:31.560
Michael slashes Laurie on the arm, which&nbsp;
causes her to stumble and fall over the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.560 --> 00:05:35.400
banister on the top floor of the Wallace&nbsp;
house. Bleeding and dizzy from the fall,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.400 --> 00:05:40.800
Laurie barely manages to escape. But "Halloween&nbsp;
Ends" reverses the roles and changes the outcome.

00:05:40.800 --> 00:05:45.180
In the finale of David Gordon Green's&nbsp;
trilogy, it's Corey who falls over the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.180 --> 00:05:50.280
stairway banister. He is dressed, at this point,&nbsp;
in full Michael Myers attire after stealing the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.280 --> 00:05:55.080
mask. At the end of the movie, Laurie fakes&nbsp;
her own suicide to lure Corey to her. Then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.080 --> 00:06:00.240
she reveals her trap and shoots Corey, sending him&nbsp;
over the banister of her house. The shot of him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.240 --> 00:06:05.040
falling echoes Laurie's fall in the original,&nbsp;
but this time the roles are reversed. Even if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.040 --> 00:06:10.080
Corey is not actually Michael, the visual imagery&nbsp;
of him dressed as Michael Myers serves as a role&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.080 --> 00:06:15.612
reversal for these characters, showing how much&nbsp;
Laurie has grown since the 1978 "Halloween."

00:06:15.720 --> 00:06:20.460
One of Laurie's biggest moments of victory in&nbsp;
John Carpenter's "Halloween" is when she stabs a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.460 --> 00:06:25.440
knitting needle into Michael Myers' neck after he&nbsp;
breaks into the Doyle home. After she stabs him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.440 --> 00:06:30.480
he goes down for a brief moment, incapacitated.&nbsp;
Viewers at the time surely thought maybe he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.480 --> 00:06:34.500
was dead, but "Halloween" fans these days&nbsp;
know he was just resting up for round two.

00:06:34.500 --> 00:06:38.160
In another reversal of Laurie and&nbsp;
Michael's roles as predator and prey,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.160 --> 00:06:42.120
it is not Michael who gets stabbed when&nbsp;
"Halloween Ends" eventually references the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.120 --> 00:06:46.920
knitting needle scene. During their final scuffle&nbsp;
in Laurie's home, Michael grabs a knitting needle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.920 --> 00:06:51.600
and stabs Laurie. Maybe it's payback from&nbsp;
over 40 years ago. Of course, maybe Michael&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.600 --> 00:06:55.860
is just looking for the most convenient way to&nbsp;
kill his rival. Regardless of why it happens,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.860 --> 00:07:00.746
this moment is a fun twist on a callback&nbsp;
that would otherwise be a bit too obvious.

00:07:00.840 --> 00:07:05.520
If you watched David Gordon Green's 2018&nbsp;
"Halloween," you may remember Julian. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.520 --> 00:07:09.660
is also known as the hardest kid in the&nbsp;
world to babysit. A hilarious addition to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.660 --> 00:07:14.040
"Halloween," is that Julian managed to survive&nbsp;
an attack by Michael Myers. His babysitter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.040 --> 00:07:18.360
Vicky, and her boyfriend were not as&nbsp;
lucky. Though babysitters rarely are.

00:07:18.360 --> 00:07:21.540
"You go up there, you're gonna get killed, Dave!"

00:07:21.540 --> 00:07:25.620
Julian was confirmed to have survived Myers'&nbsp;
killing spree in "Halloween," but he doesn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.620 --> 00:07:28.980
show up in "Halloween Ends" until one&nbsp;
of the final scenes. In that scene,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.980 --> 00:07:33.240
the movie confirms that Sondra lived and&nbsp;
shows other minor characters from the trilogy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.240 --> 00:07:37.260
like Lindsey Wallace, getting their final&nbsp;
vengeance on Michael. In this montage of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.260 --> 00:07:42.000
the people of Haddonfield accompanying Laurie in&nbsp;
a procession, we get a glimpse of Julian. Just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.000 --> 00:07:46.303
knowing he survived all the horrible nights&nbsp;
in Haddonfield is enough to warm our hearts.

00:07:46.440 --> 00:07:51.120
Pumpkins and pumpkin pie are pretty universal&nbsp;
symbols for fall and especially the Halloween&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.120 --> 00:07:54.540
season. The same can be said of&nbsp;
the "Halloween" movies. Pumpkins&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.540 --> 00:07:58.560
are often a symbol of the series as well,&nbsp;
appearing in the opening credits sequences&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.560 --> 00:08:02.700
of all the David Gordon Green movies as&nbsp;
an homage to the opening credits of John&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.700 --> 00:08:06.900
Carpenter's original film. This pumpkin&nbsp;
imagery comes full circle at the end.

00:08:06.900 --> 00:08:11.580
Early on in the movie, Laurie burns a pie.&nbsp;
She tells Allyson she's trying to bake her a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.580 --> 00:08:15.900
pumpkin pie because it is her favorite dessert&nbsp;
during this time of year. After all the stress&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.900 --> 00:08:20.220
of Michael's final killing spree, the serial&nbsp;
killer is supposedly dead and disposed of,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.220 --> 00:08:25.080
and we see a period of peace in Haddonfield.&nbsp;
Allyson recovers from the trauma and prepares&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.080 --> 00:08:29.340
to begin her life anew; in the background&nbsp;
of the shot sits a delicious-looking slice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.340 --> 00:08:33.163
of pumpkin pie. A fitting bit of&nbsp;
closure to her terrifying story.

